1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:05,140
Okay, moving on to acute renal failure. This is an acute severe decrease in renal function,

2
00:00:05,580 --> 00:00:11,100
the hallmark of which is azotemia. Azotemia refers to an increase in nitrogenous waste

3
00:00:11,100 --> 00:00:15,460
products in the blood, and it's commonly measured as an increase in the BUN and the creatinine.

4
00:00:15,900 --> 00:00:20,300
In addition, patients often have oliguria, and oliguria refers to low production of urine.

5
00:00:20,880 --> 00:00:24,580
Now, when a patient presents with the signs and symptoms of acute renal failure,

6
00:00:25,040 --> 00:00:28,980
we're going to divide the etiology into pre-renal, post-renal, and intra-renal.

7
00:00:28,980 --> 00:00:35,440
We can also look at it this way. Here's the kidney, and here's the blood vessel bringing blood into the kidney,

8
00:00:35,660 --> 00:00:38,200
and here's the ureter, which is draining the kidney.

9
00:00:38,860 --> 00:00:42,280
One way by which we can get acute renal failure would be to decrease the blood flow.

10
00:00:42,720 --> 00:00:47,840
A decrease in blood flow would result in acute renal failure, and we call that pre-renal azotemia.

11
00:00:48,660 --> 00:00:54,900
Another way by which we can develop acute renal failure would be to block the outflow,

12
00:00:54,900 --> 00:00:59,940
and if we block the outflow, then that would result in a back pressure in the kidney,

13
00:01:00,080 --> 00:01:05,040
which would decrease the GFR of the kidney, which would then, of course, result in acute renal failure.

14
00:01:05,360 --> 00:01:07,940
We call that post-renal azotemia.

15
00:01:08,360 --> 00:01:12,220
And finally, we could have problems within the kidney that could result in renal failure,

16
00:01:12,600 --> 00:01:15,080
and we'd call that intra-renal azotemia.

17
00:01:15,740 --> 00:01:20,880
Focusing on each of these individually, pre-renal azotemia is due to a decrease in blood flow to the kidneys,

18
00:01:20,880 --> 00:01:22,360
as I've already just illustrated.

19
00:01:22,800 --> 00:01:25,340
This is a relatively common cause of acute renal failure,

20
00:01:25,820 --> 00:01:28,460
and the decreased blood flow is going to result in decreased GFR.

21
00:01:28,980 --> 00:01:31,560
Now, of course, if there's decreased blood going to the glomerulus,

22
00:01:31,720 --> 00:01:35,220
then there will be less blood filtered by the glomerulus, and so the GFR would go down.

23
00:01:35,660 --> 00:01:38,880
In addition, we'd get buildup of nitrogenous waste products, or azotemia,

24
00:01:39,380 --> 00:01:41,920
and a decrease in the production of urine, or oliguria.

25
00:01:42,580 --> 00:01:47,660
Now, whenever a patient develops azotemia, both the BUN and the creatinine in the blood are going to rise.

26
00:01:47,660 --> 00:01:55,700
However, we can use the ratio of the BUN to creatinine to try to help us nail down the exact cause of the azotemia.

27
00:01:56,220 --> 00:01:59,660
For example, in the particular case of pre-renal azotemia,

28
00:02:00,120 --> 00:02:03,980
the serum BUN to creatinine ratio will rise to greater than 15.

29
00:02:04,720 --> 00:02:07,760
Let's illustrate this just to ensure that we understand the principle.

30
00:02:08,180 --> 00:02:13,140
Here you have a tubule, and of course, BUN and creatinine are both filtered by the glomerulus.

31
00:02:13,140 --> 00:02:17,300
Now, normally, what would happen is that some BUN would return to the blood.

32
00:02:17,640 --> 00:02:20,360
However, creatinine does not have the ability to be resorbed.

33
00:02:20,760 --> 00:02:25,520
So, therefore, the normal BUN to creatinine ratio is approximately 15.

34
00:02:26,360 --> 00:02:30,540
Now, in the case of pre-renal azotemia, because there's low blood flow to the kidney,

35
00:02:31,080 --> 00:02:33,420
the renin-angiotensin system will be activated.

36
00:02:33,820 --> 00:02:35,960
And when the renin-angiotensin system is activated,

37
00:02:36,120 --> 00:02:40,300
one of the consequences will be the release of aldosterone from the adrenal gland.

38
00:02:40,300 --> 00:02:44,820
Of course, aldosterone will come back to the kidney and will cause resorption of sodium.

39
00:02:45,280 --> 00:02:47,500
And along with sodium, eventually, you'll resorb water.

40
00:02:47,900 --> 00:02:51,500
Now, as you resorb water, additional BUN will flow into the blood.

41
00:02:51,820 --> 00:02:53,760
And as additional BUN flows into the blood,

42
00:02:54,080 --> 00:02:57,860
the BUN to creatinine ratio will now become greater than 15.

43
00:02:58,420 --> 00:03:02,120
And this is a particularly high-yield point to be aware of.

44
00:03:02,400 --> 00:03:04,380
And that's why I spent some time illustrating it.

45
00:03:04,680 --> 00:03:06,540
So, let's go back, and what we say here in the text,

46
00:03:06,540 --> 00:03:13,160
we say that resorption of fluid in BUN will ensue, resulting in a BUN to creatinine ratio greater than 15.

47
00:03:13,800 --> 00:03:19,120
Now, recall that in this particular case of pre-renal azotemia, we haven't damaged the tubules at all.

48
00:03:19,560 --> 00:03:22,400
And so, therefore, the tubular function will remain intact.

49
00:03:22,840 --> 00:03:26,420
And so, the FINA, or the fractional excretion of sodium, will be less than 1%,

50
00:03:26,420 --> 00:03:28,880
indicating that the tubules are able to resorb sodium.

51
00:03:29,460 --> 00:03:32,720
And similarly, the urine osmolality will be greater than 500,

52
00:03:32,860 --> 00:03:36,120
indicating that the tubules have the ability to concentrate the urine.

53
00:03:36,540 --> 00:03:40,680
In post-renal azotemia, you have obstruction of the urinary tract downstream from the kidney.

54
00:03:41,380 --> 00:03:45,100
This decreased outflow results in a back pressure, which then decreases the GFR.

55
00:03:45,520 --> 00:03:47,060
We can illustrate this as follows.

56
00:03:47,180 --> 00:03:51,580
If this is the glomerulus here, and this is the tubule draining the glomerulus,

57
00:03:52,120 --> 00:03:56,400
if there is a back pressure going this way, and there is a glomerular pressure going this way,

58
00:03:56,640 --> 00:03:59,160
this back pressure will decrease the glomerular pressure,

59
00:03:59,420 --> 00:04:01,680
so that you won't be able to filter as much.

60
00:04:01,680 --> 00:04:05,940
And so, again, decreased outflow in the back pressure will result in a decreased GFR.

61
00:04:06,360 --> 00:04:10,540
If there is a decreased GFR, we'll also develop azotemia, as is stated here,

62
00:04:10,620 --> 00:04:13,080
because nitrogenous waste products will build up in the blood,

63
00:04:13,500 --> 00:04:17,620
and we'll also develop oliguria, because, again, we won't be producing as much urine.

64
00:04:18,240 --> 00:04:21,380
Now, in the very early stages of a post-renal azotemia,

65
00:04:21,860 --> 00:04:25,380
there will be increased tubular pressure, which will force BUN back into the blood.

66
00:04:25,380 --> 00:04:28,980
And this is going to increase the BUN to creatinine ratio.

67
00:04:29,360 --> 00:04:30,600
Again, let's illustrate this.

68
00:04:30,640 --> 00:04:36,480
Here's the tubule, and you're normally filtering both BUN and creatinine into the filtrate.

69
00:04:36,860 --> 00:04:40,960
Now, because there's going to be a back pressure coming from the obstruction,

70
00:04:41,360 --> 00:04:45,060
this back pressure will force additional BUN out into the blood.

71
00:04:45,380 --> 00:04:49,980
And so the BUN to creatinine ratio in the blood will now be greater than 15.

72
00:04:49,980 --> 00:04:54,300
At the same time, it's important to note that the tubular function remains intact

73
00:04:54,300 --> 00:04:58,100
during the early stage of post-renal azotemia.

74
00:04:58,680 --> 00:05:00,960
And so, therefore, the phenol will be less than 1%,

75
00:05:00,960 --> 00:05:03,600
and the uranosmolality will be greater than 500,

76
00:05:03,880 --> 00:05:06,360
both indicating that the tubules are functioning fine.

77
00:05:06,880 --> 00:05:10,980
If there's long-standing obstruction, however, eventually the tubule will get damaged.

78
00:05:11,360 --> 00:05:14,620
And when the tubule gets damaged, you're going to get decreased resorption of BUN,

79
00:05:15,020 --> 00:05:18,840
because the resorption of BUN is dependent on an intact tubule.

80
00:05:18,840 --> 00:05:22,880
Therefore, the serum BUN to creatinine ratio will now be less than 15.

81
00:05:23,360 --> 00:05:24,660
Let's go back and illustrate this.

82
00:05:24,720 --> 00:05:28,740
If this is the tubule, and you've got this epithelium here, which is lining the tubule,

83
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:32,180
you need the epithelium to be intact in order to resorb BUN.

84
00:05:32,660 --> 00:05:35,080
So as BUN comes in and creatinine comes in,

85
00:05:35,380 --> 00:05:38,920
you would normally resorb BUN so that the ratio would be 15.

86
00:05:39,460 --> 00:05:43,280
However, in this particular case, you're not going to be able to resorb as much BUN,

87
00:05:43,280 --> 00:05:48,080
and so, therefore, the BUN to creatinine ratio will now become less than 15.

88
00:05:48,080 --> 00:05:50,000
And so that's important to note.

89
00:05:50,940 --> 00:05:52,920
Now, at the same time, because you've damaged the tubules,

90
00:05:53,020 --> 00:05:55,020
you're going to decrease the ability to resorb sodium.

91
00:05:55,440 --> 00:05:57,700
And so here, the phenol will be greater than 2%.

92
00:05:57,700 --> 00:06:00,640
In addition, you'll decrease the ability to concentrate urine,

93
00:06:00,860 --> 00:06:02,500
again, because the tubules are damaged,

94
00:06:02,880 --> 00:06:05,540
and so the urine osmolality will be less than 500.

95
00:06:06,400 --> 00:06:08,920
Now, once you've understood pre-renal and post-renal azotemia,

96
00:06:09,040 --> 00:06:12,160
we're now going to move to the intrarenal causes of azotemia.

97
00:06:12,560 --> 00:06:14,580
And the first is acute tubular necrosis.

98
00:06:14,580 --> 00:06:19,400
In this particular disorder, you get injury and necrosis of the tubular epithelial cells.

99
00:06:19,760 --> 00:06:22,100
This is the most common cause of acute renal failure.

100
00:06:22,680 --> 00:06:27,400
Now, that injury and necrosis results in necrotic cells, which then plug the tubules.

101
00:06:27,680 --> 00:06:31,780
That blocks the GFR, creating an intrarenal azotemia.

102
00:06:32,280 --> 00:06:33,500
Let's illustrate that here.

103
00:06:33,780 --> 00:06:34,620
Here's the glomerulus.

104
00:06:35,740 --> 00:06:38,360
And, of course, the glomerulus is going to filter into the tubule,

105
00:06:38,620 --> 00:06:42,480
and you're well aware of the fact that the tubule is lined by these epithelial cells.

106
00:06:42,480 --> 00:06:46,360
Now, imagine if we have acute tubular necrosis so that these cells die.

107
00:06:46,700 --> 00:06:50,980
When these cells die, they are going to slough off here into the tubule.

108
00:06:51,320 --> 00:06:54,320
And as they slough off into the tubule, they are going to block the tubule.

109
00:06:54,640 --> 00:06:57,940
When there's a block in the tubule, that's going to result in a back pressure this way,

110
00:06:58,240 --> 00:07:01,580
which is going to decrease the ability to generate a decent GFR.

111
00:07:02,120 --> 00:07:05,960
Again, the back pressure will counteract the pressure generated by the blood

112
00:07:05,960 --> 00:07:08,160
in the effort to produce a GFR.

113
00:07:08,160 --> 00:07:13,780
So, again, as I've stated here in the text, the necrotic cells are going to plug the tubules.

114
00:07:14,100 --> 00:07:16,640
That obstruction is then going to decrease the GFR.

115
00:07:17,140 --> 00:07:20,180
Another classic hallmark that would be seen in acute tubular necrosis

116
00:07:20,180 --> 00:07:23,360
is the presence of dirty brown granular casts in the urine.

117
00:07:23,820 --> 00:07:26,220
Now, again, the idea here is that if this is the tubule,

118
00:07:26,520 --> 00:07:29,060
and the tubule is normally lined by epithelial cells,

119
00:07:29,440 --> 00:07:32,400
in acute tubular necrosis, these epithelial cells, they die.

120
00:07:32,400 --> 00:07:36,240
And as they die, they come into the tubule, they obstruct the tubule,

121
00:07:36,620 --> 00:07:39,480
but then they get casted in the shape of the tubule,

122
00:07:39,640 --> 00:07:41,140
so that when they come out in the urine,

123
00:07:41,480 --> 00:07:47,300
you actually see a cast with all of these individual epithelial cells making up the cast.

124
00:07:47,800 --> 00:07:51,380
Now, those epithelial cells are dead, and so they look brown and granular,

125
00:07:51,900 --> 00:07:53,980
and so we call that a brown granular cast,

126
00:07:54,040 --> 00:07:56,120
and that's what's going to be seen in the urine.

127
00:07:56,460 --> 00:08:00,600
We can also illustrate acute tubular necrosis using this histologic image.

128
00:08:00,600 --> 00:08:05,540
Here's the glomerulus, and each of these are tubules that I'm highlighting here.

129
00:08:06,040 --> 00:08:08,420
This is probably the most normal tubule in the image,

130
00:08:08,660 --> 00:08:10,540
and notice that these cells have nuclei,

131
00:08:10,980 --> 00:08:13,780
and that the cells are up against their basement membrane.

132
00:08:14,360 --> 00:08:16,480
Now, in acute tubular necrosis, the cells die,

133
00:08:16,800 --> 00:08:20,000
and so you lose the nuclei, as you would expect in necrosis,

134
00:08:20,260 --> 00:08:24,760
and in addition, you get detachment of the cells, notice here, from their basement membrane.

135
00:08:25,160 --> 00:08:28,540
Of course, this results in sloughing of the cells into the tubule,

136
00:08:28,540 --> 00:08:33,280
which then eventually would get casted in the shape of a dirty brown granular cast.

137
00:08:33,680 --> 00:08:36,940
And also, of course, the plugging of these tubules by the necrotic cells

138
00:08:36,940 --> 00:08:39,680
would create a back pressure, which would decrease the GFR.

139
00:08:40,300 --> 00:08:44,160
Now, in this particular case, we're going to have a dysfunctional tubular epithelium,

140
00:08:44,540 --> 00:08:47,020
and so therefore, we're not going to be able to resorb BUN,

141
00:08:47,520 --> 00:08:50,200
and so the BUN to creatinine ratio will be less than 15.

142
00:08:50,600 --> 00:08:52,300
I've already illustrated this earlier.

143
00:08:52,980 --> 00:08:55,540
Also, because we don't have good tubular function,

144
00:08:55,540 --> 00:08:59,760
we're going to have decreased resorption of sodium, so the phenol will be greater than 2%,

145
00:08:59,760 --> 00:09:04,580
and an inability to concentrate the urine, so the urine osmolality will be less than 500.

146
00:09:05,240 --> 00:09:08,320
Now, when we have acute tubular necrosis, there are two major etiologies.

147
00:09:08,760 --> 00:09:11,920
There's an ischemic etiology and a nephrotoxic etiology.

148
00:09:12,480 --> 00:09:15,600
In ischemic acute tubular necrosis, we decrease the blood supply,

149
00:09:15,740 --> 00:09:17,980
and that's what results in the necrosis of the tubules.

150
00:09:18,500 --> 00:09:21,640
Now, recall that the tubules are particularly dependent on blood supply

151
00:09:21,640 --> 00:09:23,640
because of the amount of work that they have to do.

152
00:09:23,640 --> 00:09:26,880
So, there's a tremendous amount of ATP that's required by the tubule,

153
00:09:27,020 --> 00:09:29,080
especially the proximal renal tubule,

154
00:09:29,500 --> 00:09:32,340
along with the medullary segment of the thick ascending limb.

155
00:09:32,680 --> 00:09:35,020
These are particularly susceptible to ischemia.

156
00:09:35,880 --> 00:09:39,360
Now, note here one last thing, and that is, in ischemic acute tubular necrosis,

157
00:09:39,660 --> 00:09:42,160
it's usually going to be preceded by prerenal azotemia.

158
00:09:42,520 --> 00:09:45,260
You decrease the blood flow, you get a prerenal azotemia.

159
00:09:45,660 --> 00:09:48,700
As the blood flow remains decreased for an extended period of time,

160
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:52,560
that eventually progresses to ischemic acute tubular necrosis.

161
00:09:52,560 --> 00:09:59,240
In nephrotoxic acute tubular necrosis, you also get necrosis of the tubular epithelial cells.

162
00:09:59,580 --> 00:10:02,320
However, in this particular case, it's due to toxic agents.

163
00:10:02,700 --> 00:10:05,680
And again, the proximal tubule is particularly susceptible.

164
00:10:06,300 --> 00:10:10,140
Now, some of the important causes of nephrotoxic acute tubular necrosis include

165
00:10:10,140 --> 00:10:13,400
amino glycosides, and you're well aware from your pharmacology studies

166
00:10:13,400 --> 00:10:16,640
that amino glycosides can cause renal damage.

167
00:10:17,040 --> 00:10:18,040
Heavy metals can do it.

168
00:10:18,040 --> 00:10:20,940
For example, lead poisoning can cause an acute tubular necrosis.

169
00:10:21,540 --> 00:10:22,540
Myoglobinuria can do it.

170
00:10:22,620 --> 00:10:25,780
Patients who have crush injury, they release myoglobin into the blood,

171
00:10:25,840 --> 00:10:28,740
and that can damage the kidney, causing an acute tubular necrosis.

172
00:10:29,300 --> 00:10:31,100
Ethylene glycol is a classic example.

173
00:10:31,500 --> 00:10:33,800
Recall that ethylene glycol is basically antifreeze,

174
00:10:34,160 --> 00:10:38,840
and it could be taken by accident, for example, if a child were to consume it.

175
00:10:39,140 --> 00:10:41,880
Remember that ethylene glycol has a blue color and tastes sweet.

176
00:10:41,880 --> 00:10:45,980
And so sometimes children can think of it as a juice, and they might drink it,

177
00:10:46,220 --> 00:10:48,500
which would then result in an acute tubular necrosis.

178
00:10:48,840 --> 00:10:51,760
It's also important to note that patients who are exposed to ethylene glycol

179
00:10:51,760 --> 00:10:54,280
often develop oxalate crystals in their urine.

180
00:10:54,620 --> 00:10:57,080
And again, that's particularly high yield for examinations.

181
00:10:57,640 --> 00:11:00,720
Radiocontrast dye can also create an acute tubular necrosis.

182
00:11:01,020 --> 00:11:02,940
And then finally, urate can also do it.

183
00:11:03,300 --> 00:11:06,860
Now, the classic example of a urate nephropathy would be

184
00:11:06,860 --> 00:11:08,880
where patients undergo tumor lysis syndrome.

185
00:11:08,880 --> 00:11:12,540
So, for example, if a patient has an acute leukemia,

186
00:11:12,680 --> 00:11:13,980
and you treat them with chemotherapy,

187
00:11:14,460 --> 00:11:16,760
that chemotherapy is going to rapidly kill

188
00:11:16,760 --> 00:11:20,980
the large amount of cells that are floating around in the blood.

189
00:11:21,340 --> 00:11:25,500
And so that rapid death of the cells results in rapid breakdown of nuclear material,

190
00:11:25,660 --> 00:11:27,380
which increases the uric acid levels.

191
00:11:27,720 --> 00:11:32,300
Of course, that uric acid could then damage the tubules via acute tubular necrosis.

192
00:11:32,760 --> 00:11:35,620
Therefore, in order to avoid this tumor lysis syndrome,

193
00:11:36,100 --> 00:11:37,960
what we often do is we hydrate the patients

194
00:11:37,960 --> 00:11:40,080
to make sure that there's good flow going through the urine.

195
00:11:40,440 --> 00:11:42,960
And we can also use allopyrinol, of course,

196
00:11:43,040 --> 00:11:45,980
to prevent the formation of uric acid.

197
00:11:46,620 --> 00:11:49,940
Now, clinically, when patients have an acute tubular necrosis,

198
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:51,020
you're going to get oliguria,

199
00:11:51,200 --> 00:11:54,400
again, because you've blocked the ability of the kidney

200
00:11:54,400 --> 00:11:57,260
to generate a decent GFR by plugging the tubules.

201
00:11:57,600 --> 00:11:59,940
At the same time, you're going to see brown granular casts

202
00:11:59,940 --> 00:12:02,780
in the urine, as I've already illustrated.

203
00:12:03,360 --> 00:12:06,200
At the same time, we're going to see brown granular casts in the urine,

204
00:12:06,480 --> 00:12:07,680
as I've already illustrated.

205
00:12:08,200 --> 00:12:10,440
Now, the BUN and creatinine will both rise.

206
00:12:10,680 --> 00:12:13,080
Of course, if you're not able to create a GFR,

207
00:12:13,220 --> 00:12:16,240
you're not going to be able to filter out the BUN and creatinine from the blood.

208
00:12:16,480 --> 00:12:18,740
And finally, the patients will develop hyperkalemia

209
00:12:18,740 --> 00:12:21,000
with a metabolic acidosis.

210
00:12:21,340 --> 00:12:22,860
The hyperkalemia is due to the fact

211
00:12:22,860 --> 00:12:25,720
that you get decreased renal excretion of potassium.

212
00:12:26,140 --> 00:12:28,340
And the metabolic acidosis is due to the fact

213
00:12:28,340 --> 00:12:30,760
that you get decreased excretion of organic acids.

214
00:12:31,260 --> 00:12:33,680
And so, actually, this acidosis would be associated

215
00:12:33,680 --> 00:12:35,640
with an increased anion gap.

216
00:12:36,080 --> 00:12:38,260
Now, acute tubular necrosis is reversible.

217
00:12:38,840 --> 00:12:41,480
The tubular cells do have the ability to regenerate.

218
00:12:41,900 --> 00:12:44,480
However, this often requires supportive dialysis

219
00:12:44,480 --> 00:12:48,540
because the electrolyte imbalances generated during ATN can be fatal.

220
00:12:48,960 --> 00:12:50,160
It's important to note, however,

221
00:12:50,240 --> 00:12:52,240
that the allegoria that the patients experience

222
00:12:52,240 --> 00:12:54,540
can actually persist for two to three weeks

223
00:12:54,540 --> 00:12:55,660
before they actually recover.

224
00:12:56,140 --> 00:12:58,160
Now, the reason for this is that the tubular cells,

225
00:12:58,260 --> 00:13:01,140
especially the proximal tubular cell, is a stable cell.

226
00:13:01,460 --> 00:13:04,880
And remember from our earlier discussions from general pathology

227
00:13:04,880 --> 00:13:07,680
that stable cells are cells that have the ability

228
00:13:07,680 --> 00:13:10,940
to reenter cell cycle but are currently out of cell cycle.

229
00:13:11,320 --> 00:13:13,560
And so, it takes a little bit of time for the tubular cell

230
00:13:13,560 --> 00:13:17,160
to actually reenter cell cycle and then eventually regenerate.

231
00:13:17,680 --> 00:13:20,460
Another important cause of intrarenal azotemia

232
00:13:20,460 --> 00:13:22,420
is acute interstitial nephritis.

233
00:13:22,880 --> 00:13:25,220
Now, the word interstitium implies that we're dealing

234
00:13:25,220 --> 00:13:27,300
with the connective tissue between the tubules.

235
00:13:27,420 --> 00:13:28,660
That's what interstitium is.

236
00:13:28,720 --> 00:13:31,200
It's all that connective tissue between the tubules.

237
00:13:31,500 --> 00:13:32,620
And, of course, there's inflammation.

238
00:13:33,340 --> 00:13:36,240
This is basically a drug-induced hypersensitivity reaction

239
00:13:36,240 --> 00:13:37,160
of the interstitium.

240
00:13:37,540 --> 00:13:39,320
It can spread over into the tubules.

241
00:13:39,760 --> 00:13:41,520
The key causes are going to be drugs.

242
00:13:41,520 --> 00:13:45,360
They include NSAIDs, penicillin, and diuretics,

243
00:13:45,420 --> 00:13:46,280
just to list a few.

244
00:13:46,740 --> 00:13:48,340
Here's an image just highlighting the idea.

245
00:13:48,660 --> 00:13:51,200
Notice that these are tubules, and the tubules look fine.

246
00:13:51,580 --> 00:13:53,740
However, what's going on here is that you've got

247
00:13:53,740 --> 00:13:56,300
an inflammatory infiltrate in the connective tissue

248
00:13:56,300 --> 00:13:58,500
or the interstitium between the tubules.

249
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:01,400
This inflammatory infiltrate will then result in

250
00:14:01,400 --> 00:14:02,840
an acute renal failure.

251
00:14:03,400 --> 00:14:05,860
Now, the presentation is oliguria, again,

252
00:14:05,860 --> 00:14:09,580
because there is a renal failure with fever and rash

253
00:14:09,580 --> 00:14:13,660
that arises days to weeks after starting the inciting drug.

254
00:14:14,120 --> 00:14:17,260
One of the very high-yield, almost pathognomonic findings

255
00:14:17,260 --> 00:14:20,940
of acute interstitial nephritis is that eosinophils

256
00:14:20,940 --> 00:14:23,220
will be present, and those eosinophils may be seen

257
00:14:23,220 --> 00:14:23,720
in the urine.

258
00:14:24,220 --> 00:14:26,240
Of course, this is a hypersensitivity reaction,

259
00:14:26,400 --> 00:14:29,060
so it will resolve with cessation of the drug.

260
00:14:29,440 --> 00:14:32,000
However, it can progress to renal papillary necrosis

261
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:34,720
where the papillae of the kidney actually necrose.

262
00:14:35,480 --> 00:14:37,960
Now, in renal papillary necrosis, again,

263
00:14:38,100 --> 00:14:40,220
you get necrosis of the renal papillae.

264
00:14:40,460 --> 00:14:42,920
This presents with gross hematuria and flank pain,

265
00:14:43,320 --> 00:14:45,360
and there are a few important causes to be aware of.

266
00:14:45,620 --> 00:14:47,460
The first is chronic analgesic abuse.

267
00:14:47,600 --> 00:14:49,900
For example, long-term finacetin or aspirin

268
00:14:49,900 --> 00:14:52,880
can result in a renal papillary necrosis.

269
00:14:53,240 --> 00:14:56,660
Other causes include diabetes, sickle cell disease or trait,

270
00:14:56,660 --> 00:14:58,840
and severe acute pyelonephritis.

271
00:14:58,840 --> 00:15:02,200
So this concludes our section on acute renal failure.

